WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.945
Suppose a California city clerk expects two hundred permit requests this week.

2
00:00:03.945 --> 00:00:08.699
Each trained clerk can process forty requests, but one clerk is already assigned to elections.

3
00:00:08.699 --> 00:00:12.947
How many additional clerks are needed, and what assumptions hide inside that answer?

4
00:00:12.947 --> 00:00:17.195
Algebra turns the staffing question into quantities, relationships, and constraints.

5
00:00:17.195 --> 00:00:18.510
It does not decide policy.

6
00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:21.696
It helps us say clearly what follows from the numbers we chose.

7
00:00:21.696 --> 00:00:23.428
This is study preparation only.

8
00:00:23.428 --> 00:00:26.781
Listening earns no score, course credit, or transfer credit.

9
00:00:26.781 --> 00:00:32.759
Work the examples on paper, and verify any credit plan directly with the institution that would receive it.

10
00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:34.163
Begin with number sense.

11
00:00:34.163 --> 00:00:35.509
A fraction is division.

12
00:00:35.509 --> 00:00:41.303
Three fourths of forty requests means forty divided into four equal groups, then take three groups.

13
00:00:41.303 --> 00:00:43.000
Forty divided by four is ten.

14
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:44.521
Three times ten is thirty.

15
00:00:44.521 --> 00:00:49.788
A negative number can represent a decrease, a debt, or a position below a reference point.

16
00:00:49.788 --> 00:00:57.219
If a department is five hours under budget and then saves three more hours, its difference from budget is negative eight hours.

17
00:00:57.219 --> 00:01:01.257
Order of operations keeps one expression from having several answers.

18
00:01:01.257 --> 00:01:09.450
Grouping comes first, then exponents, then multiplication and division from left to right, then addition and subtraction from left to right.

19
00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:11.381
Consider six plus two times five.

20
00:01:11.381 --> 00:01:13.663
Multiply two by five first, giving ten.

21
00:01:13.663 --> 00:01:15.360
Then add six, giving sixteen.

22
00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:20.861
If the intended quantity is six plus two, all multiplied by five, the grouping must be stated.

23
00:01:20.861 --> 00:01:23.962
Six plus two is eight, and eight times five is forty.

24
00:01:23.962 --> 00:01:26.478
Exponents compress repeated multiplication.

25
00:01:26.478 --> 00:01:29.580
Three squared means three times three, which is nine.

26
00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.511
It does not mean three times two.

27
00:01:31.511 --> 00:01:34.963
A square root reverses squaring when the context allows it.

28
00:01:34.963 --> 00:01:39.937
The principal square root of forty-nine is seven because seven squared is forty-nine.

29
00:01:39.937 --> 00:01:41.108
Now translate words.

30
00:01:41.108 --> 00:01:45.614
"Five more than twice a number" becomes two times the number, then plus five.

31
00:01:45.614 --> 00:01:46.784
Let the number be x.

32
00:01:46.784 --> 00:01:49.183
The spoken expression is two x plus five.

33
00:01:49.183 --> 00:01:54.567
"At most one hundred requests" means the request count is less than or equal to one hundred.

34
00:01:54.567 --> 00:02:00.536
Translation is often the hardest step, so name the quantity and its unit before manipulating anything.

35
00:02:00.536 --> 00:02:03.871
An equation states that two expressions have equal value.

36
00:02:03.871 --> 00:02:06.387
Solve three x plus six equals twenty-seven.

37
00:02:06.387 --> 00:02:08.436
First subtract six from both sides.

38
00:02:08.436 --> 00:02:09.957
Three x equals twenty-one.

39
00:02:09.957 --> 00:02:11.830
Then divide both sides by three.

40
00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:12.707
x equals seven.

41
00:02:12.707 --> 00:02:14.756
Check it in the original statement.

42
00:02:14.756 --> 00:02:19.027
Three times seven is twenty-one, and twenty-one plus six is twenty-seven.

43
00:02:19.027 --> 00:02:29.619
The check catches arithmetic slips and answers the question, "Does this value actually satisfy the equation?" For a formula with several letters, the same balance principle applies.

44
00:02:29.619 --> 00:02:35.295
Suppose total staff hours, H, equal five hours of setup plus two hours for each service batch, b.

45
00:02:35.295 --> 00:02:37.987
Spoken as a formula, H equals five plus two b.

46
00:02:37.987 --> 00:02:41.440
If total hours are twenty-one, substitute twenty-one for H.

47
00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:43.254
Subtract five, leaving sixteen.

48
00:02:43.254 --> 00:02:44.073
Divide by two.

49
00:02:44.073 --> 00:02:46.004
The office handled eight batches.

50
00:02:46.004 --> 00:02:49.236
Inequalities describe ranges rather than single values.

51
00:02:49.236 --> 00:02:52.468
Solve four x plus eight is less than or equal to forty.

52
00:02:52.468 --> 00:02:54.290
Subtract eight from both sides.

53
00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:56.816
Four x is less than or equal to thirty-two.

54
00:02:56.816 --> 00:02:57.698
Divide by four.

55
00:02:57.698 --> 00:02:59.637
x is less than or equal to eight.

56
00:02:59.637 --> 00:03:02.810
Every value at or below eight satisfies the condition.

57
00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:05.043
One rule deserves deliberate practice.

58
00:03:05.043 --> 00:03:09.450
Multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative reverses the direction.

59
00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:12.153
Solve negative three x is greater than twelve.

60
00:03:12.153 --> 00:03:17.853
Divide by negative three and reverse "greater than" to "less than." x is less than negative four.

61
00:03:17.853 --> 00:03:18.970
Test negative five.

62
00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:24.905
Negative three times negative five is fifteen, and fifteen is greater than twelve, so the test works.

63
00:03:24.905 --> 00:03:27.079
Test zero and it fails, as it should.

64
00:03:27.079 --> 00:03:31.839
Absolute value measures distance from zero, so absolute value cannot be negative.

65
00:03:31.839 --> 00:03:35.893
If the absolute value of x is six, then x can be six or negative six.

66
00:03:35.893 --> 00:03:41.006
If the absolute value of x is less than three, x lies between negative three and three.

67
00:03:41.006 --> 00:03:48.351
In civic data, an absolute difference can describe how far an estimate missed without labeling the miss positive or negative.

68
00:03:48.351 --> 00:03:52.053
A function assigns exactly one output to every permitted input.

69
00:03:52.053 --> 00:03:54.521
The domain is the permitted set of inputs.

70
00:03:54.521 --> 00:03:56.930
The range is the set of outputs produced.

71
00:03:56.930 --> 00:04:00.397
Imagine cost as a function of completed inspection batches.

72
00:04:00.397 --> 00:04:04.863
Cost equals one hundred dollars of setup plus twenty-five dollars per batch.

73
00:04:04.863 --> 00:04:07.155
Zero batches costs one hundred dollars.

74
00:04:07.155 --> 00:04:12.032
Four batches costs one hundred plus four times twenty-five, or two hundred dollars.

75
00:04:12.032 --> 00:04:17.145
That function is linear because equal changes in input produce equal changes in output.

76
00:04:17.145 --> 00:04:19.672
Its slope is twenty-five dollars per batch.

77
00:04:19.672 --> 00:04:24.784
Its vertical intercept is one hundred dollars, the modeled cost when the input is zero.

78
00:04:24.784 --> 00:04:29.661
Slope has units: dollars per batch, minutes per call, or residents per square mile.

79
00:04:29.661 --> 00:04:33.011
A bare slope without units can hide a bad interpretation.

80
00:04:33.011 --> 00:04:34.950
Find slope from two observations.

81
00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:38.358
At two batches, modeled cost is one hundred fifty dollars.

82
00:04:38.358 --> 00:04:41.179
At six batches, it is two hundred fifty dollars.

83
00:04:41.179 --> 00:04:45.527
Output changed by one hundred dollars while input changed by four batches.

84
00:04:45.527 --> 00:04:49.112
One hundred divided by four is twenty-five dollars per batch.

85
00:04:49.112 --> 00:04:50.992
The same rate confirms the line.

86
00:04:50.992 --> 00:04:52.872
The domain must respect reality.

87
00:04:52.872 --> 00:04:54.753
Negative batches are impossible.

88
00:04:54.753 --> 00:04:57.397
A city may also cap the shift at ten batches.

89
00:04:57.397 --> 00:05:01.040
Then a sensible domain is whole numbers from zero through ten.

90
00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:05.154
The algebraic line continues forever, but the staffing model does not.

91
00:05:05.154 --> 00:05:09.326
Beyond ten batches, overtime or another shift may change the cost rule.

92
00:05:09.326 --> 00:05:11.683
Two linear equations can form a system.

93
00:05:11.683 --> 00:05:14.039
The solution must satisfy both at once.

94
00:05:14.039 --> 00:05:19.961
Suppose a California county schedules standard appointment blocks and extended appointment blocks.

95
00:05:19.961 --> 00:05:21.592
There are ten blocks total.

96
00:05:21.592 --> 00:05:25.761
A standard block uses one staff hour, and an extended block uses two.

97
00:05:25.761 --> 00:05:27.937
The office has fourteen staff hours.

98
00:05:27.937 --> 00:05:30.958
Let s be standard blocks and e be extended blocks.

99
00:05:30.958 --> 00:05:33.617
The first equation says s plus e equals ten.

100
00:05:33.617 --> 00:05:36.336
The second says s plus two e equals fourteen.

101
00:05:36.336 --> 00:05:38.994
Subtract the first equation from the second.

102
00:05:38.994 --> 00:05:41.291
The s terms cancel, and e equals four.

103
00:05:41.291 --> 00:05:43.768
Substitute four into s plus e equals ten.

104
00:05:43.768 --> 00:05:46.185
s plus four equals ten, so s equals six.

105
00:05:46.185 --> 00:05:49.387
Check: six standard plus four extended is ten blocks.

106
00:05:49.387 --> 00:05:51.744
Six hours plus eight hours is fourteen.

107
00:05:51.744 --> 00:05:53.073
Both constraints hold.

108
00:05:53.073 --> 00:05:57.545
A system can have one solution, no solution, or infinitely many solutions.

109
00:05:57.545 --> 00:06:00.203
Two lines that cross once give one solution.

110
00:06:00.203 --> 00:06:04.131
Parallel lines give no solution because the constraints conflict.

111
00:06:04.131 --> 00:06:08.361
Two equations describing the same line give infinitely many solutions.

112
00:06:08.361 --> 00:06:14.645
In budgeting, "no solution" may mean the promised service level and available hours cannot both be true.

113
00:06:14.645 --> 00:06:16.941
That is a finding, not a math failure.

114
00:06:16.941 --> 00:06:18.814
Return to the opening question.

115
00:06:18.814 --> 00:06:24.071
Two hundred requests divided by forty requests per clerk equals five clerk assignments.

116
00:06:24.071 --> 00:06:32.289
One clerk is available only after election work, so the phrase "additional clerks" depends on whether that clerk is counted in the five.

117
00:06:32.289 --> 00:06:38.090
If one clerk can process permits and four more are needed, the answer is four additional clerks.

118
00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:42.501
If the election assignment makes that clerk unavailable, five are needed.

119
00:06:42.501 --> 00:06:44.313
Algebra exposes the ambiguity.

120
00:06:44.313 --> 00:06:46.066
Policy staff must resolve it.

121
00:06:46.066 --> 00:06:47.576
Practice one final model.

122
00:06:47.576 --> 00:06:53.437
A California town charges a twelve-dollar records fee plus three dollars for each certified page.

123
00:06:53.437 --> 00:06:59.842
For seven pages, multiply three by seven, giving twenty-one, then add twelve, giving thirty-three dollars.

124
00:06:59.842 --> 00:07:07.154
If a resident can spend no more than thirty-nine dollars, solve twelve plus three p is less than or equal to thirty-nine.

125
00:07:07.154 --> 00:07:08.121
Subtract twelve.

126
00:07:08.121 --> 00:07:10.900
Three p is less than or equal to twenty-seven.

127
00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:11.867
Divide by three.

128
00:07:11.867 --> 00:07:14.163
p is less than or equal to nine pages.

129
00:07:14.163 --> 00:07:15.152
Three questions.

130
00:07:15.152 --> 00:07:19.788
First, why does dividing an inequality by a negative reverse its direction?

131
00:07:19.788 --> 00:07:24.054
Second, what do slope and intercept mean in the permit cost function?

132
00:07:24.054 --> 00:07:26.712
Third, what must a solution to a system do?

133
00:07:26.712 --> 00:07:31.688
First answer: reversing the direction preserves which values make the comparison true.

134
00:07:31.688 --> 00:07:38.804
For example, negative five is less than negative two, but after multiplying both by negative one, five is greater than two.

135
00:07:38.804 --> 00:07:44.069
Second answer: slope is the added cost for one more batch, with units of dollars per batch.

136
00:07:44.069 --> 00:07:46.962
The intercept is the modeled cost at zero batches.

137
00:07:46.962 --> 00:07:53.037
Third answer: a system solution must satisfy every equation or inequality in the system at the same time.

138
00:07:53.037 --> 00:08:04.602
Artifact: complete a one-page mixed set with fractions, signed numbers, order of operations, equations, inequalities, functions, slopes, and one two-equation system.

139
00:08:04.602 --> 00:08:13.644
Show each step, include units, check every solution in the original statement, and write one sentence explaining each correction.

140
00:08:13.644 --> 00:08:20.583
Source note: Original study audio based on public learning materials, without quoting source prose.

